      "latency_ms": 2148.9,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 508,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two types of atoms: **hydrogen and oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$H_2O$**—meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\nHowever, to humans, Earth, and all known life, water is much more than just a chemical formula. Here is a breakdown of what water is, why it is unique, and why it is so important.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Three States of Matter\nWater is famous for being the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct physical states:\n*   **Liquid:** The flowing water we drink, swim in, and use for oceans, rivers, and rain.\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Frozen water found in glaciers, snow, ice cubes, and frost.\n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Invisible water vapor in the air, clouds, and steam from boiling water.\n\n### 2. Unique Chemical Properties\nWater has several bizarre and unique scientific properties that make life possible:\n*   **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Because of its electrical polarity (one side is slightly positive, the other slightly negative), water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is how it transports vital nutrients and minerals through our bodies and through ecosystems.\n*   **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing insects to walk on water) and to other surfaces (adhesion—which helps water travel up the roots of giant trees).\n*   **Ice Floats:** Almost all substances shrink and become denser when they freeze. Water is different; when it freezes, it expands and becomes *less* dense. This is why ice floats on water. If ice sank, lakes and oceans would freeze from the bottom up, destroying all aquatic life.\n*   **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps human bodies from overheating.\n\n### 3. Water on Earth\n*   **The Blue Planet:** About **71% of Earth’s surface** is covered by water, mostly in its oceans.\n*   **Scarcity:** Despite the planet looking so blue, about **96.5% of Earth's water is salt water** in the oceans."
